  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nPatchy fog may develop amongst the river valleys and lower \nelevations around and east of Billings this morning. The latest \nHRRR shows this with reduced visibilities. Will continue to \nmonitor this through the morning hours.\n\nSoutheasterly surface winds will spread across the area today,\nbringing in moisture from the Plains. Aloft, an upper shortwave\nwill move through the region this afternoon ahead of a parent\nupper low. Isolated to scattered showers and a few weak \nthunderstorms are in the forecast over the mountains of \nsouthwestern Montana as well as across southeastern Montana and \nnorthern Wyoming. The chance for precipitation across south- \ncentral Montana is lower, with weaker forcing available. A few \nstronger thunderstorms are possible over southeastern Montana, \ncapable of producing gusty winds and heavy rainfall.\n\nPrecipitable water values will increase today and linger through\nthe weekend as the parent low moves into the region. The latest\nensemble guidance depicts PWAT values exceeding 200% of normal by\nFriday. This will bring the risk of very heavy rainfall with\nshowers and thunderstorms Friday through Saturday. There is a risk\nfor a few stronger thunderstorms on Friday, with the low digging\nthrough eastern Idaho. Model soundings depict long and skinny CAPE\nprofiles for much of the area, though the best instability is\nshown mainly east of Billings. Weaker shear could hinder stronger\nconvection though, along with weaker daytime heating. The latest \nSPC outlook highlights much of Montana and Wyoming in a General \nThunderstorm Outlook for now, but continue to monitor the forecast\nfor potential updates. \n\nThe main concerns with showers and thunderstorms Friday and\nSaturday will be erratic wind gusts and very heavy rainfall. \nWidespread rainfall is currently forecast, with the probability \nfor at least 1 inch of rainfall at 40 to 80%, greatest across \nsouth-central Montana and the Bighorn Mountains. WPC has a \nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall over the area both Friday and\nSaturday now. So, plan on a wet weekend, especially Saturday, if \nyou have outdoor plans and ave a way to get weather alerts in case\nflood advisories or warnings become necessary. \n\nUncertainty remains with the progression of this system over the \nweekend. The latest GFS ensemble has trended closer to what the\nECMWF ensemble has been showing, with the upper low sliding \neastward through Sunday. Previous runs of the GEFS were moving the\nsystem out of the region faster. Widespread precipitation looks \nto persist through Sunday mainly across southeastern Montana and \nnorthern Wyoming. Deviations to the track could keep precipitation\nfurther west into the mountains and foothills west of Billings.\nHowever, the latest trends show precipitation ending by Sunday \nafternoon for locations around and west of Billings at this time.\n\nThis system does not look to be very cold, so the chance for\nmountain snow is not as high as last weekends system. However,\nseveral inches of snowfall are in the latest forecast for the\nhighest elevations of the Absaroka/Beartooths and Crazy mountains\nthis weekend. Will continue to monitor this situation to see if \nimpacts to the Beartooth Highway would necessitate an advisory, \nbut at this time it does not appear an advisory will be needed.\n\nMatos/Chambers",
